atorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [17592186262878 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)-3\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t+1}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t+1}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{3(t+1)}\\right),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3(t+1)^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(0)-3\\mathbf{g}(0))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(0)-3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3(0)^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{0+1}⟩-3⟨-\\frac{1}{3(0+1)^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}0,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩-3⟨-\\frac{1}{3},1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩-⟨-1,3,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262892 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))-2(4\\cdot 3-(-5)\\cdot (-1))+3(4\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12-14+30 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|28|=28.\n$$"] [17592186262893 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 9) \\\\  & =14+12 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|26|=26.\n$$"] [17592186262894 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)-(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 3)+4(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-3k+1+32 \\\\  & =33-3k \\\\  & =3(11-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 3(11-k) & =0 \\\\ 11-k & =0 \\\\ k & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=11.$"] [17592186262897 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{f}(t)-\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\sqrt{3-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3-t}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\pi t,\\hspace{0.167em}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{f}(-1)-\\mathbf{g}(-1))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(-1)-{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(-1) \\\\  & =2⟨-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3-(-1)}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(-\\pi ),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{(-1)}⟩-⟨2(-1)-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\pi (-1),\\hspace{0.167em}-2(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨-2,-\\pi ,-1⟩-⟨-3,-2\\pi ,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2\\pi ,-2⟩-⟨-3,-2\\pi ,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262898 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & t & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t(2\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 6)-t(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 6)+(3\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-24t+18t+6 \\\\  & =-6t+6 \\\\  & =6(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 6(1-t) & =0 \\\\ 1-t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=1.$"] [17592186262899 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 7 \\\\ t & 4 & t \\\\ 2 & 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & t \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(4\\cdot 8-6\\cdot t)+(t\\cdot 8-2\\cdot t)+7(t\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =96-18t+8t-2t+42t-56 \\\\  & =30t+40 \\\\  & =10(3t+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 10(3t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186262900 "By the sum rule, we have \n \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{2}f(t)+5\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+5{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}(2\\sqrt{3+t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+t}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence , we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{f}(1)+5\\mathbf{g}(1))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(1)+5{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(1) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+1}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(1-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2(1) \\\\ 2(1+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262905 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{tan}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{tan}t-\\operatorname{sec}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{tan}t-\\operatorname{sec}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262907 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(8\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+9\\mathbf{k})-(7\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,5⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(7\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(7\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(7\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-2))+(0\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-2))+5(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2-4-20 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-22|=22.\n$$"] [17592186262909 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{t}⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}⟨\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}4t\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\sqrt{t}⟩+⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}t\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.167em}5t\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}3\\sqrt{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262910 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =(1-t)⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩-⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}1+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-t,\\hspace{0.167em}1-t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-2{t}^{2}⟩-⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}1+t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2t,\\hspace{0.167em}-2t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-3{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262911 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1+2t⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =2t⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1+2t⟩+2⟨4\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}t+{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}2t+4{t}^{2}⟩+⟨8\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}2t+2{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.167em}8{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}4t+6{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262912 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1)-0+3((-2)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2-12 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-10|=10.\n$$"] [17592186262914 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{4}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}⟨-2{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{4}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩+t⟨2{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩+⟨2t{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}4t\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t{e}^{-t}-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t+4t\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t{e}^{-t}(2-t),\\hspace{0.167em}2t(1+2\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262923 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}2u),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{tan}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}u⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}(u(t)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u(t)},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}(u(t))⟩\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}({t}^{2})⟩\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}({t}^{2})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262924 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{f}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2({e}^{t}) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262925 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}=v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩ \\\\ {v}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & =v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t) \\\\  & =2t⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩+2⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩+⟨2\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{ln}t+1,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262926 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(u\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)+{u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{2}⟩+3{t}^{2}⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}⟩+⟨3{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}5{t}^{4},\\hspace{0.167em}12{t}^{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262930 "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}=v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t+1}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ {v}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & =v(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{v}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{2t}⟩+{e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{-t},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2{e}^{3t}⟩+⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}1,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{3t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}3{e}^{3t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265867 "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(2,1) & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(2,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(2,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265868 "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(0,-1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(0,-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265869 "The directional derivative ${f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(a,b)=\\mathrm{∇}f(a,b)\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\mathbf{v}‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2(1)-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\mathbf{u}}^{'}(1,1) & =\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,1)\\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
